.\" Manual page for workforest(1). Hand-written roff (man macros): no build
.\" step, so the same file ships in the wheel, the AUR package, and the
.\" Homebrew formula. tests/test_man.py keeps it in sync with cli.py.
.\" wf.1 is a .so link to this page; workforest.5/wf.5 cover the config files.
.TH WORKFOREST 1 "" workforest "User Commands"
.SH NAME
workforest, wf \- git worktree forest management
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY workforest
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.YS
.SY "workforest create"
.RI [ BRANCH ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR OPENER ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR WRAPPER ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-hooks ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-open ]
.YS
.SY "workforest open"
.RI [ NAME ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR OPENER ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR WRAPPER ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR PATH ]
.YS
.SY "workforest list"
.RB [ \-\-porcelain ]
.YS
.SY "workforest delete"
.IR NAME .\|.\|.
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-delete\-branch " | " \-\-keep\-branch ]
.YS
.SY "workforest checkout"
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.YS
.SY "workforest run"
.RB [ \-b ]
.I SCRIPT
.RI [ ARGS .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY "workforest stop"
.I SCRIPT
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.YS
.SY "workforest tui"
.RI [ MODE ]
.YS
.SY "workforest init"
.RB [ \-\-local ]
.YS
.SY "workforest config"
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.YS
.SY "workforest shell-init"
.RB [ bash | zsh ]
.YS
.SY "workforest claude copy-session"
.I SESSION_ID
.YS
.SY workforest
.I OPENER
.RI [ NAME ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR PATH ]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B workforest
manages a
.IR forest :
one main checkout of a git repository plus any number of disposable,
per-branch worktrees in a predictable location, each created, set up,
opened, and cleaned up with one command.
.PP
.EX
~/dev/
|\-\- api/                  # main checkout
\(ga\-\- worktrees/
    \(ga\-\- api/
        |\-\- feature\-x/    # wf create feature\-x
        \(ga\-\- fix\-y/
.EE
.PP
A worktree's
.I name
is the last path component of its branch
.RB ( feature/x " \(-> " x ),
which is also its directory name under the worktrees directory.
Only worktrees located directly inside that directory are
.IR managed ;
every command operates on managed worktrees of the repository that
contains the current directory.
.PP
.B wf
is an alias for
.BR workforest .
After
.B shell-init
(see
.BR "SHELL INTEGRATION" )
it is a shell function that can change the calling shell's directory.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS create [\fIBRANCH\fR]
Create (or reuse) a worktree for
.I BRANCH
and open it.
.I BRANCH
defaults to the current branch.
It is resolved in order: an existing local branch; a branch on exactly
one remote (the worktree tracks it); otherwise a brand-new local branch.
.I REMOTE/BRANCH
picks the remote explicitly, which is required when several remotes
carry the same branch name; when that local name is already taken,
.B create
prompts for a different one.
.PP
In a fresh worktree the project's
.B symlinks
are created and its
.B setup_scripts
run (see
.BR workforest (5))
before the opener starts.
A branch that is already checked out somewhere is opened there instead.
.TP
.BR \-o " \fIOPENER\fP, " \-\-opener " \fIOPENER\fP"
An
.B openers
name from the configuration, or a shell command.
Default: the
.B opener
key, then
.BR $VISUAL ,
then
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.BR \-w " \fIWRAPPER\fP, " \-\-wrap " \fIWRAPPER\fP"
Run the opener through this
.B wrappers
entry, overriding the opener's own
.B wrap
setting.
An empty value
.RB ( "\-w ''" )
runs it without any wrapper.
.TP
.BR \-p " \fIPATH\fP, " \-\-path " \fIPATH\fP"
Path inside the worktree, passed to the opener as
.BR $WF_TARGET .
Default:
.BR . .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-hooks
Skip symlinks and setup scripts.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-open
Create only; do not open.
.SS open [\fINAME\fR]
Open an existing worktree with an opener.
Without
.IR NAME ,
open the managed worktree the current directory is in.
Takes the same
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-w ,
and
.B \-p
options as
.BR create .
.SS list
List managed worktrees: name, branch, clean/dirty state, and path.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Stable machine-readable output, one worktree per line:
.IR NAME ,
.IR BRANCH ,
.IR PATH ,
and
.B 1
(dirty) or
.B 0
(clean), separated by tabs.
.SS delete \fINAME\fR...
Delete one or more worktrees.
Every name is resolved before anything is removed, so a typo fails the
whole batch.
A worktree with uncommitted changes is listed and confirmed first;
afterwards, on a terminal, you are asked whether to delete its branch
too.
Deleting the worktree you are standing in moves the shell back to the
main checkout.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Skip the dirty-worktree confirmation.
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-branch
Also delete the branch, without asking.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-branch
Never delete the branch.
.SS checkout \fINAME\fR
Collapse a worktree back into the main checkout: delete it, then check
its branch out in the main worktree and move the shell there.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Skip the dirty-worktree confirmation.
.SS run [\fB\-b\fR] \fISCRIPT\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
Run the named script from the merged configuration's
.B scripts
map, from the root of the current worktree.
Extra arguments are shell-quoted and appended to the script command (a
group \(em a
.B bulk
or
.B pipeline
entry, see
.BR workforest (5)
\(em takes none).
See
.B ENVIRONMENT
for the variables scripts receive.
.PP
The command runs in a process group of its own, in the foreground of the
terminal, so Ctrl-C and Ctrl-Z reach it directly; SIGINT, SIGTERM, and
SIGHUP sent to
.B workforest
itself are forwarded to the whole group.
The script's
.B cleanup
command (see
.BR workforest (5))
then runs however the command ended.
Starting an
.B exclusive
script first stops its running instance in any worktree of the project
and waits for that instance's cleanup; any other script refuses to start
while it is already running in this worktree (see
.BR stop ).
The exit status is the command's, or 128+\fIN\fR when it was killed by
signal
.IR N .
A command killed by SIGINT \(em or exiting 130, as a shell does once its
child was \(em is reported as interrupted, a warning rather than an
error, and ends
.B workforest
by SIGINT as well, so a shell loop around it aborts.
A group runs under a supervisor that takes the command's place in all of
the above: a pipeline runs its members in order and ends with the first
failing step's status; a bulk runs them at once, relays their output with
the member's name as a line prefix, and ends with the first failing
member's status once all have ended.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-background
Detach instead: the command runs under a supervisor of its own with its
output in
.IR .git/workforest/logs/ SCRIPT / WORKTREE .log
of the main checkout, and
.B workforest
returns once it is clearly running (a command that dies right away is
reported with the tail of its log).
The
.B background
entry key does the same by default.
Must precede
.IR SCRIPT ;
after it, it belongs to
.IR ARGS .
.SS stop \fISCRIPT\fR [\fB\-\-all\fR]
Stop the script's running instance in the current worktree \(em
foreground or background, started from any terminal \(em with SIGTERM,
then SIGKILL after
.B stop_timeout
seconds, and wait for its cleanup to finish.
For a group this stops every member and waits for all of their cleanups,
then the group's own; a member can also be stopped by name out from
under a running group.
Fails when nothing is running.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Every worktree's instance of the script, not just this one's.
.SS tui [\fIMODE\fR]
Interactive mode, driven by
.BR fzf (1).
Running
.B workforest
with no arguments at all is the same as
.BR "workforest tui" .
.I MODE
is the initial tab:
.BR create ,
.BR open ,
.BR checkout ,
or
.B delete
(plus
.B claude
when the integration is active); the default is
.B open
when the forest has worktrees, else
.BR create .
.PP
Keys:
.B Left
/
.B Right
(or
.BR Alt\-h " / " Alt\-l )
switch tabs;
.B Ctrl\-Left
/
.B Ctrl\-Right
cycle the opener used by
.B create
and
.BR open ;
.B Enter
accepts the selection (in
.BR create ,
a query that matches no branch names a new branch);
.B Esc
quits.
The
.B delete
tab stays open after each deletion for bulk cleanup.
.SS init
Write a short, fully commented
.B .workforest.yaml
starter in the main checkout. Every key is commented out, so the file
changes nothing until edited; see
.BR workforest (5)
for the reference.
.TP
.B \-\-local
Place it in
.B .vscode/
or
.B .idea/
instead: an untracked, per-developer override.
.SS config
Show the merged configuration as YAML, followed by the files each layer
came from.
Works outside a repository too (project layers are then absent).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON object with
.B config
and
.B sources
keys instead.
.SS shell-init [bash|zsh]
Print the shell integration for
.BR bash " or " zsh
(default: from
.BR $SHELL ).
See
.BR "SHELL INTEGRATION" .
.SS claude copy-session \fISESSION_ID\fR
Copy a Claude Code session from the main worktree into the current one.
The
.B claude
command exists only when
.B ~/.claude
is present.
It is experimental: it edits Claude Code's private on-disk state, which
is not a stable interface, so any Claude Code update may break it.
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
A subprocess cannot change its parent shell's directory, so add to your
.BR ~/.bashrc " or " ~/.zshrc :
.PP
.EX
eval "$(workforest shell-init)"
.EE
.PP
This defines
.B wf
as a shell function that runs
.B workforest
and evaluates the directive it prints (a
.B cd
into the worktree, followed by the opener command); registers tab
completion for both
.BR wf " and " workforest ;
and, when workforest was installed into a Python environment whose
manual pages are not on the default search path (a
.B uv tool
or
.B pipx
install), prepends that location to
.BR $MANPATH .
.PP
Without the integration everything still works, but an opener that runs
in your terminal prints the
.B cd
command instead of performing it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Configuration is read from files and flags only; nothing in the
environment changes the merged result.
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
The default opener when neither
.B \-o
nor the
.B opener
key is set.
.TP
.B SHELL
Runs openers, wrappers, and scripts
.RB ( "$SHELL \-c" ),
and selects the dialect for
.B shell-init
when no argument is given.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base of the user configuration directory (default
.BR ~/.config ).
.TP
.BR NO_COLOR ", " CLICOLOR_FORCE
Disable or force colored messages on stderr.
.TP
.B WORKFOREST_DEBUG
When set, errors re-raise with a Python traceback instead of a message.
.PP
Openers, wrappers, setup scripts, and named scripts run with the
.B WF_*
family in their environment:
.TP
.B WF_MAIN
Path of the main worktree.
.TP
.B WF_NAME
Repository name (the main checkout's directory name).
.TP
.B WF_WORKTREES_DIR
The resolved worktrees directory.
.TP
.B WF_WORKTREE
Path of the worktree being opened or run in.
.TP
.B WF_BRANCH
Its branch (empty when detached).
.TP
.B WF_TARGET
The
.B \-p
argument, default
.B .
(openers and wrappers only).
.TP
.B WF_TITLE
A window label,
.RI \(dq NAME ": " BRANCH \(dq
(openers and wrappers only).
.TP
.B WF_ENV
All of the above as shell-quoted
.I NAME=value
assignments, for re-creating the family where the environment is not
inherited (openers and wrappers only).
.TP
.B WF_COMMAND
In a wrapper: the opener command, unexpanded.
.PP
See
.BR workforest (5)
for how these are used in configuration.
.SH FILES
Later layers override earlier ones; see
.BR workforest (5).
.TP
.I /etc/workforest/config.yaml
System configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/workforest/config.yaml
User configuration.
.TP
.I .workforest.yaml
Shared project configuration, in the repository root.
.TP
.IR .vscode/.workforest.yaml ", " .idea/.workforest.yaml
Local (untracked) project configuration.
.PP
Each may also be a
.B .yml
or
.B .json
file; the first existing basename wins.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Cancelled at a prompt, or a prompt was needed but no terminal was
available (use
.BR \-\-force ).
.TP
.B 4
Configuration error.
.TP
.BI 128+ N
The
.B run
command was killed by signal
.IR N .
.PP
Human-readable messages go to stderr.
Stdout carries only machine output: directives for the
.B wf
shell function, listings, and configuration dumps.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B wf create feature/login
Create a worktree for the branch, run the project's setup hooks, and open
it in
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.B wf open login \-o lazygit
Open the
.B login
worktree with any command.
.TP
.B wf run make check \-j2
Run the
.B make
script with extra arguments appended.
.TP
.B wf checkout login
Fold the branch back into the main checkout.
.TP
.B wf delete fix-y \-\-delete\-branch
Remove a worktree and its branch.
.TP
.B wf
Start the interactive TUI.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR workforest (5),
.BR git-worktree (1),
.BR fzf (1)
.PP
.UR https://github.com/ArkadyBuryakov/workforest
.UE
